				"location": "Tokyo Japan",
				"description": "On the morning of 10th December 1968, a young man wearing the uniform of a motorcycle police officer pulled over black Nissan Cedric luxury sedan carrying four employees of the <i>Nihon Shintaku Ginko</i> (Nippon Trust Bank) and three metal cases containing ¥294,307,500.<br><br>The police officer told the car's occupants that he had just been informed that their bank and the bank manager's house had been blown up, and that there was an explosive device planted in their vehicle. The four men exited the car while the police officer searched beneath the car. When flames and smoke billowed from the car the police officer warned them that the car was going to explode. The four men dashed to find cover behind a wall. Peeking over the wall they saw the policeman get in the car and drive away. When there was no explosion the four men found what looked like a warning flare in the road. They also noticed that the motorcycle was a crudely-made replica.<br><br>Despite the efforts of the Tokyo Metropolitan Police Department the thief escaped after transferring the boxes to a previously stolen car, which he also later abandoned after transferring the boxes to yet another stolen car. The police launched a the largest investigation in Japanese history. Lasting seven years the investigation involved around 170,000 officers. In 1988 the statute of limitations passed, meaning that the thief could no longer be tried. The crime remains unsolved.",
